From 82cbdd4b512aa426b2f64ca34316d29e3974ec46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:59:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: printf formats no longer hide the
 printed command; pin `function NAME {` (PR #2298 review nits)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Under an output sink, `_iter_printed_shell_payloads` walks the argument
line of every executed `echo`/`printf` as shell code. Two shapes slipped
past it, so the guard returned (False, None) while bash ran an oversized,
unscannable script (review 5892927210 on PR #2298; both pre-existing,
not #2298 regressions):

1. `function f { echo bash /x/huge.sh; }; f | bash` (also `echo -n`,
   `time -p echo …`). Main 2a35439ef1 ("one keyword walk") has since
   fixed the peel itself: the printed-payload loop now uses
   `_peel_reserved_words`, which drops `function NAME`, `time -p` and
   `in`. This commit keeps that and only pins the shapes in tests.
2. `printf '%s %s' bash /x/huge.sh | bash` (also `printf '%s ' …`,
   `printf -- '%s %s' …`, `printf 'ba%s %s' sh …`, `printf "$f" …`). The
   line was walked as `%s %s bash /x/huge.sh`, so `%s` read as the
   command; only formats containing `\n` split by luck.

Fix (fail closed; every new variant is only more text walked as executed):
- For printf, `_printed_argument_lines` also yields the arguments without
  the format (covers formats the walk cannot read, e.g. "$f") and
  `_printf_output(format, arguments)`: each conversion takes the next
  argument as text, `%%` is `%`, and the format repeats while arguments
  remain, capped at 64 rounds (the format-less variant still holds every
  argument past the cap).
- The printf model lives in the new sibling `cron/lifecycle_guard_printf.py`
  (`_printf_format_lines`, `_printf_output`) so `cron/lifecycle_guard.py`
  stays at its 2000-line FILE_LINES cap.
- Known remaining gap (pre-existing, follow-up t_01a1d58e0438e52e): `*`
  width, `.N` precision and `%b` escapes are not modelled.

Tests:
- `_SINK_SHAPES` gains echo-function-keyword, echo-n-function-keyword,
  echo-function-keyword-parens (pinned), echo-time-p,
  printf-format-two, printf-format-trailing-space, printf-dashdash-format,
  printf-format-split-word, printf-format-function-keyword,
  printf-variable-format, printf-format-repeats and echo-after-in;
  `_NO_SINK_SHAPES` gains the function-keyword and printf-format shapes
  with no sink (still allowed). Unit tests pin `_printf_output` and its
  round cap, and that an argument-less printer (`printf --`, `printf`,
  `echo -n`) does not crash the walk.
- Corpus: SINK_CONSUMERS gains `printf-format` (`printf '%s ' {line}`)
  and SINK_NESTINGS gains `function-keyword` (the producer on the
  `function f {` line). main_verdicts.json carries the new cells.

Proof (rebased onto main 806283b0b6+, conflict in the printed-payload
loop resolved by keeping main's `_peel_reserved_words` line and dropping
this branch's `_peel_printer_segment`):
- tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_output_sink.py +
  test_lifecycle_guard_heredoc_walk.py (corpus digest included):
  359 passed; the sink file against main's guard: 13 failed (7 printf
  sink shapes + 6 `_printf_output` unit tests), 93 passed.
- scripts/check --base origin/main: 19 checks ok.
- scripts/run_tests.sh tests/cron/ plus every test file importing the
  guard: 225 files, 2908 passed, 2 skipped.
- Pre-rebase head e858ae4219 was reviewed (t_01a1d5859d900242): 16 sink
  tests red on base, 3 mutants killed.
